{%- comment -%}
  BMP global loader — the single entry point for the BuildMyPOD layer.
  Rendered from layout/theme.liquid inside the BMP-HOOK:globals block.
  Loaded AFTER Horizon's stylesheets/fonts/
  variables so BMP CSS wins by source order, never by !important.

  This render line is LOAD-BEARING: losing it silently
  un-styles the theme — pre-commit and the visual-regression canaries guard
  it. Everything BMP adds globally (styles, scripts, widgets) is wired
  here — never directly in core files.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  Preload: bmp-styles.css is above-the-fold global chrome, so fetch it at high
  priority (emits <link rel=preload as=style> + the stylesheet link) alongside
  Horizon's base.css. Precedent: snippets/stylesheets.liquid uses the same
  `stylesheet_tag: preload: true` on base.css. NOT deferred — deferring it
  would flash the whole page unstyled.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'bmp-styles.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag: preload: true }}
{% comment %}
  Global product-card style tokens (Plan 1: Title + Price). A settings-driven
  :root token block, same shape as Horizon's own head-emitted settings vars,
  PLUS the token-gated rules for typography, case and weight — those cannot be
  static, because their baseline is a per-instance preset class no var()
  fallback can reproduce (rationale in the snippet's own doc block and in the
  GLOBAL CARD STYLE section of bmp-styles.css).
  Layout rules DO stay static in bmp-styles.css.
  Placed AFTER the stylesheet link, and that ordering is LOAD-BEARING, not just
  tidy: the gated rules tie the always-on card rules on specificity ((0,2,1)
  title / (0,1,1) price) and win only by source order. Do not move this render
  above the link.
{% endcomment %}
<style>{% render 'bmp-card-style-vars' %}</style>
{%- comment -%}
  Global CTA colors. The snippet owns its conditional <style> wrapper
  and emits zero bytes while all six settings are blank. Each property is
  independent: a blank Add to Cart global releases the native block Custom
  value for that property, while configured globals take precedence. Buy Now
  is scoped to Shopify's unbranded button so wallet branding stays untouched.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- render 'bmp-cta-button-style-vars' -%}
{%- comment -%}
  a11y-slideshow-aria: keep the slideshow pagination dots/thumbnails on the
  VALID `aria-current` attribute. The snippet markup + bmp-styles.css already
  use aria-current, but the core slideshow.js controller (kept byte-identical)
  writes the invalid `aria-selected` at runtime on slide change; this module
  mirrors it back to aria-current and strips the invalid attribute. Global
  because slideshow-controls renders on many surfaces (hero, PDP gallery,
  zoom dialog, predictive carousel). fetchpriority=low — a post-paint a11y
  correction that never blocks render.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'bmp-aria-current.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-size-guide-portal: keeps the size-guide
  chart openable from inside quick-add's popup by guaranteeing its dialog is a
  <body> child rather than a DOM descendant of the open quick-add dialog
  (nesting breaks both the event routing and the fixed-position containing
  block — see the asset header); relocates the trigger out of a swatch-app-
  hidden variant picker; and applies the size-guide block's link text (published
  as a data attribute on the dialog element) to the trigger. The icon is not its
  job — a :has() rule in the trigger's stylesheet resolves that with no JS. Global, and NOT loaded from blocks/bmp-size-guide.liquid,
  because the surface that needs it most is the collection/search page, where
  that block never renders — quick-add clones the trigger in from a fetched
  product page, and a <script> inside morph-injected markup does not execute.
  fetchpriority=low, and it stays accurate: the trigger is gated server-side on
  the option name and revealed by CSS, so it is present and readable in the
  first paint without this module, icon included. What arrives late is a
  CUSTOMISED label, and only on stores that changed it.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'bmp-size-guide-portal.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-dialog-close-guard: routes a NATIVE dialog close request - the
  Android back button, which Chrome sends through CloseWatcher straight to the
  native <dialog> close with no keydown - back into the owning component's own
  close method, and releases the scroll lock outright when nothing can stop
  that close. Without it the lock acquired in dialog.js's showDialog() /
  theme-drawer.js's open() is never released, html[scroll-lock] stays on, and
  the page is frozen until a reload. Zero core JS is
  patched: the guard only calls core's public closeDialog() / close(), the same
  way bmp-card-hover.js calls previewVariant() / resetVariant(). Global and
  UNCONDITIONAL because every dialog surface is affected - cart drawer, search
  modal, quick-add, size guide, zoom, filters drawer - and the two listeners
  are document-level, so surfaces injected later (quick-add clones, filter
  morphs) are covered with no per-instance wiring. fetchpriority=low: it only
  runs on a close request, never during first paint.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'bmp-dialog-close-guard.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-drawer-focus-scope: releases the global focus trap while a drawer is
  showing a NON-modal dialog. At >= 990px core theme-drawer.js opens the panel
  with show() - the page beside it stays visible and interactive - but still
  installs focus.js's trap, a capturing document `focusin` listener that pulls
  focus back into the panel from anywhere on the page. In the header that is
  fatal: the yank blurs the open submenu, core closes it, and a closed submenu
  is inert, so the pressed link or group toggle stops being hit-testable
  between mousedown and mouseup and the click lands on an ancestor instead.
  It also leaves keyboard users unable to Tab out of a dialog that is not
  modal. Modal mode below 990px is untouched: there the native top-layer trap
  applies and is correct. Zero core JS is patched - the module only calls
  core's exported removeTrapFocus(), the same way bmp-dialog-close-guard.js
  calls closeDialog() / close(). Global because every theme-drawer surface is
  affected - cart, filters, chat. fetchpriority=low: it only runs when a
  drawer opens or the viewport crosses the breakpoint, never during first paint.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'bmp-drawer-focus-scope.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-product-labels: keeps the overlay label in sync with the selected variant
  on both the product page and product cards, by riding the same
  StandardEvents.productSelect the stock product-price component uses. Global
  because labels render on every card surface as well as the product page.
  fetchpriority=low — it only runs on a variant change, never during first
  paint. Self-gated: the module is inert on pages with no label element.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'bmp-product-labels.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-card-hover: applies the server-computed hover-target map from
  snippets/bmp-card-hover.liquid to core's own public previewVariant()/
  resetVariant() (assets/product-card.js) — never patches core JS. Global,
  not rendered from the card itself, because cards render on collection,
  search, home and recommendation surfaces alike, and only delegated
  document-level listeners cover cards injected later by infinite scroll,
  filter morphs and quick-add, with no per-card wiring. Gated on
  settings.bmp_card_hover_effect so it emits ZERO bytes at the theme default
  ('zoom', stock behavior) — it only has anything to do at 'second_image' or
  'next_variant'.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if settings.bmp_card_hover_effect == 'second_image' or settings.bmp_card_hover_effect == 'next_variant' -%}
  <script src="{{ 'bmp-card-hover.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- endif -%}
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-header-menu: drives the content-sized desktop nav dropdown
  card rendered by snippets/bmp-nav-dropdown.liquid — click to open, outside
  click / Escape / second click to close, plus the collapsible level-3 groups.
  Global and delegated on document, NOT a custom element and NOT loaded from
  the snippet: the nodes it drives live inside the header's
  data-hydration-key subtree, which is re-rendered through the Section
  Rendering API right after load, diffed by morph.js, re-rendered on every
  theme-editor change, and re-slotted by overflow-list on resize. Delegated
  listeners registered once at import survive all of that with no
  re-initialisation (R5) — which is exactly what v1.1.5 got wrong by binding
  per-node listeners in layout/theme.liquid. Unconditional: the markup it
  drives is unconditional too. fetchpriority=low — it only runs on a click or
  a keypress, never during first paint.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'bmp-header-menu.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-size-required: Layer 1 of the forced-size-selection gate — it
  refuses an add-to-cart interaction until the shopper has deliberately chosen a
  size, and flips the state attributes the presentations in bmp-styles.css key
  off. The render half is snippets/variant-main-picker.liquid
  #size-required-unselected; Layer 2 (the no-JS floor) is
  blocks/buy-buttons.liquid#size-required-layer2.

  Global and delegated, NOT a custom element and NOT loaded from the product
  template: quick-add fetches the product page and lifts product-form-component
  into its modal after load (assets/quick-add.js:214-256), and variant changes
  morph subtrees, so only listeners registered once at import cover every
  surface with no re-initialisation.

  Bound to `window` in capture phase rather than `document`, because Horizon's
  own on:submit/on:click dispatcher is ALREADY a document capture listener
  (assets/component.js:239-323, installed from Component.connectedCallback) and
  snippets/scripts.liquid loads product-form.js AHEAD of this file — so a
  document listener of ours would fire second, after handleSubmit had already
  sent the fetch. window is the first node in the capture path.

  Gated on settings.bmp_size_required so an unchecked box emits ZERO bytes, the
  discipline bmp-card-hover.js already follows. fetchpriority=low — it only runs
  on a click, change or submit, never during first paint.

  `!= false`, not a truthiness test: an unmaterialised checkbox reads as nil,
  the schema default is true (D3), and a store that never opened the Theme
  Editor still renders the gate — so nil has to load the module, and a plain
  `if settings.bmp_size_required` would skip it and leave that store with the
  render and Layer 2 but no Layer 1. Same normalisation as
  snippets/bmp-size-required-state.liquid and
  snippets/variant-main-picker.liquid, so all three halves agree.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if settings.bmp_size_required != false -%}
  <script src="{{ 'bmp-size-required.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>
{%- endif -%}
{%- comment -%}
  The manifest render emits NOTHING (the snippet is one whole-file trimmed
  comment) — it keeps the deployed theme carrying the grep-able contract and
  theme check quiet about bmp-manifest being orphaned.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- render 'bmp-manifest' -%}
{%- comment -%}
  bmp-structured-data: SEO JSON-LD (WebSite + SearchAction). Self-gated to
  request.page_type == 'index' inside the snippet, so it adds zero bytes on
  every other page. The Phase-6 opt-in was approved by the owner 2026-08-03 —
  see also snippets/bmp-org-sameas.liquid,
  injected into the core Organization node in sections/header.liquid
  (BMP-HOOK:org-sameas), which keeps SEO structured data to exactly ONE
  Organization node site-wide.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- render 'bmp-structured-data' -%}
{%- comment -%}
  Intentionally absent (vs the official BMP layer this loader is adapted from):
  - bmp-style-vars: superseded — the settings-driven card tokens now ship as
    bmp-card-style-vars (rendered above, Plan 1), and optional CTA tokens ship
    through bmp-cta-button-style-vars.
{%- endcomment -%}
